```java
@Repository  // optional with Spring Data JPA
public interface UserRepository extends CrudRepository<User, Long> {
    // All CRUD methods inherited automatically!
    // Spring generates the implementation at runtime
}

// In Service:
@Service
public class UserService {
    @Autowired
    private UserRepository userRepo;

    public User createUser(User user) {
        return userRepo.save(user);       // INSERT
    }

    public Optional<User> getUser(Long id) {
        return userRepo.findById(id);     // SELECT by PK
    }

    public List<User> getAllUsers() {
        return (List<User>) userRepo.findAll(); // SELECT *
    }
}
```

```java
// SimpleJpaRepository.save() simplified:
public <S extends T> S save(S entity) {
    if (entityInformation.isNew(entity)) {
        em.persist(entity);  // INSERT
        return entity;
    } else {
        return em.merge(entity);  // UPDATE
    }
}

// isNew() default: ID is null or 0
User newUser = new User(); // ID null → save() does INSERT
newUser.setName("Alice");
User saved = userRepo.save(newUser); // saved has auto-generated ID

User existingUser = userRepo.findById(1L).get(); // ID = 1
existingUser.setName("Bob");
userRepo.save(existingUser); // ID not null → save() does UPDATE
```

```java
// BAD: can throw NoSuchElementException
User user = userRepo.findById(id).get(); // DON'T DO THIS

// GOOD: orElseThrow for REST APIs
User user = userRepo.findById(id)
    .orElseThrow(() -> new UserNotFoundException("User not found: " + id));

// GOOD: orElse for default value
User user = userRepo.findById(id)
    .orElse(User.guest());

// GOOD: map for transformation
String name = userRepo.findById(id)
    .map(User::getName)
    .orElse("Unknown");
```

```java
// SimpleJpaRepository.deleteById simplified:
public void deleteById(ID id) {
    // 1. Load the entity (triggers L1 cache, lifecycle listeners)
    T entity = findById(id).orElseThrow(
        () -> new EmptyResultDataAccessException(...);
    );
    // 2. Delete it
    delete(entity);
}
```

This means `deleteById` fires `@PreRemove` lifecycle callbacks and cascade removals properly. If you do `DELETE FROM users WHERE id = ?` in native SQL, you bypass all of this.
